<p>Romania’s Parliament voted 281-4 on May 5 to remove Prime Minister Ilie Bolojan, bringing down his pro-European coalition less than a year after it took office. The Social Democratic Party, or PSD, which left the governing coalition in late April, and the nationalist Alliance for the Unity of Romanians, or AUR, filed the no-confidence motion. Lawmakers from Bolojan’s National Liberal Party, Save Romania Union and the ethnic Hungarian UDMR abstained. The vote followed months of disputes over austerity measures aimed at narrowing Romania’s budget deficit, which several accounts described as the largest in the European Union. The government’s measures included tax increases, public-sector wage and pension freezes, spending cuts and reductions in public administration jobs. President Nicusor Dan said negotiations to form a new government were underway, ruled out early elections and said Romania would have a pro-Western government after the procedures concluded.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/q9f6pbp04xie9XAsnUMmZ9Cs4vTd7pbqghDz3MXL7sMF9XSGmS3NDGoOASvidP1d_oTJbCkuqtU5vFOkJGO5ZndAAEDWL3iYZVp_AaZBNnojUiYggvpet7YEBzBDSW8kvCe4ZaX9Vyt_9QKjxRIGL1R0MT0NFAk8WSNzvWjSNSkpFX6ifJijf4k_-4YSnWi1vV8vqeTwSCupTAnwgch0sCe4nfFeuDiDAHYfAqGnV8wREIGzLNg91pQ7RQ' alt='Romanian Prime Minister Ilie Bolojan during the no-confidence vote in the Romanian Parliament in Bucharest, May 5, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Motion signatures: Before the vote, 254 of Romania’s 465 lawmakers had signed the no-confidence proposal, according to Kurier.</li><li>Economic backdrop: Euronews described Romania as also grappling with rampant inflation and a technical recession.</li><li>Opposition breadth: El País reported that PSD voted alongside AUR as well as the SOS and POT parties, extending support beyond the two parties that filed the motion.</li><li>PSD posture: Although PSD voted with AUR to topple Bolojan, it had not ruled out joining a new pro-European coalition and had rejected governing with AUR.</li></ul><blockquote>Can anyone say how Romania will function from tomorrow, do you have a plan? - Ilie Bolojan</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ilie Bolojan: Bolojan called the motion &quot;cynical and artificial&quot; and said he had chosen to do what was &quot;urgent and necessary&quot; for Romania despite expecting no applause from citizens. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-05/world-news/Romania-s-prime-minister-fights-for-survival-as-no-confidence-motion-is-debated-in-Parliament-6736289403'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li><li>President Nicusor Dan: Dan said the government’s fall was &quot;not a happy moment&quot; but a democratic decision by Parliament, and he urged Romanians to remain calm. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-06/world-news/Romania-s-pro-European-coalition-collapses-after-prime-minister-fails-a-no-confidence-vote-6736289426'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li><li>AUR leader George Simion: Simion welcomed the government’s fall on X, saying the past 10 months had brought &quot;taxes, war and poverty,&quot; and called for snap elections. (<a href='https://www.letemps.ch/articles/en-roumanie-le-gouvernement-pro-europeen-tombe-apres-l-adoption-d-une-motion-de-censure-par-le-parlement'>Le Temps</a>)</li><li>Party of European Socialists: PES Secretary-General Giacomo Filibeck said Romania needed a government that protected citizens from economic uncertainty, strengthened social justice and restored trust in democratic institutions. (<a href='https://www.eunews.it/en/2026/05/05/the-pro-european-bolojan-government-has-fallen-in-romania/'>Eunews</a>)</li><li>Political scientist Costin Ciobanu: Ciobanu said PSD’s cooperation with AUR turned the nationalist party into a major political actor after other parties had previously ostracized it. (<a href='https://www.letemps.ch/articles/en-roumanie-le-gouvernement-pro-europeen-tombe-apres-l-adoption-d-une-motion-de-censure-par-le-parlement'>Le Te